# agent-sandbox environment

## Running commands
{{if .Hook}}- Run shell commands with your normal Bash/Monitor tools; a PreToolUse hook transparently routes each one through the sandbox.
- Command output is returned inline in the tool result.
{{else}}- The Bash and Monitor tools are disabled; use the `run_command` MCP tool to run shell commands.
- Command output is written to files; read the returned stdout/stderr paths.
{{end}}
There is no shell in the loop. The command broker parses your command line
itself (pipelines, `&&`/`||`/`;`, redirections, globbing, `$(…)`, `for`/`if`,
`cd` and the other shell builtins all work), and executes each simple command
directly — never through `bash` or `sh`. The broker never dispatches a shell
itself, so nothing it runs hands you one directly.

{{if or .PolicyCommands .FloorPaths}}Every command in your line sorts into one of two tiers:

- **Policy commands** run in their own child sandbox, with their own
  filesystem/network grants. Some carry nono's own argv rules directly
  (`invocation_policy`); others are instead bound to a `safe <tool>`
  wrapper (`git` is one, in the current profile) that parses the real
  invocation in Go and decides there, with the real binary reachable only
  from that wrapper. Either way, the broker dispatches to a policy
  command only through nono's own generated shim — never by an absolute
  path, a symlink, or any other indirection that skips it. That guarantee is
  about how the broker itself dispatches; it is not a guarantee about what a
  *different* command's own sandbox can still reach and run — see below.
- **Floor commands** run directly in the broker's own sandbox, with no argv
  rules of their own.

A program in neither tier is never dispatched by the broker — refused
before it starts, the same way an unknown command would be. This allowlist
is absolute about *what the broker itself will dispatch*, not about which
programs can execute: it says nothing about what a command the broker does
dispatch can go on to do once it runs. See the next paragraph.

A toolchain in either tier that can compile and immediately execute code you
give it is bounded only by what *its own* sandbox can reach — not by argv
rules, and not by which other tools are or are not enumerated elsewhere in
this profile — and it can run whatever those grants reach, including a copy
of a program neither tier names, made and executed entirely inside its own
sandbox without ever going through the broker's dispatch or nono's shim. If
such a command is listed below, its own grants (not this document's prose
about tiers) are the real boundary; ask the operator what those are before
assuming a two-tier model holds for everything it might do.

A wrapper's own rule list (below) is a Go-side denylist on the parsed
invocation, not a complete boundary by itself: it cannot see a route that
reaches the same effective setting by writing straight to a file under
`$WORKDIR` instead of going through the tool's own CLI — that write is one
of this broker's own shell builtins (see further down), never the wrapper's
parser, never nono's shim. What actually bounds what a setting reached that
way can *do* is the policy command's own sandbox grants for the real
binary — in particular a narrow `exec_paths`, which blocks shelling out to
run something regardless of whether the wrapper's own denylist happens to
name that setting. Do not treat a wrapper's rule list as the whole story
for a command with real, file-backed ambient state (git's repository config
is the case this profile has); ask what that command's own `exec_paths`
and filesystem grants actually allow before assuming the parser is holding
something back that a narrower sandbox grant is really the one enforcing.

