A refusal reaches you one of two ways. A wrapper-bound command's own check
prints `blocked: <reason>` and exits 1 — the wrapper ran and refused, in Go,
before nono was ever involved. Everything else nono itself refuses — an
`invocation_policy` denial, or a command absent from both tiers — carries a
reason (or, for a command in neither tier, none at all) on stderr and exits
126. Only the *second* shape with **no** reason at all means the command is
missing from both tiers; don't retry it, ask the operator to add it to the
command profile instead.

One thing the two tiers don't cover: the broker's own shell builtins (`echo`,
`cd`, `test`, `read`, and the others `mvdan.cc/sh` implements) run inside the
broker itself, at the broker's own filesystem grants — they never reach either
tier. A redirect or glob you write is bounded by those same grants, not by
whichever command's output it's attached to.
{{else}}Every command runs in the broker's own sandbox. There are no tiers and
no allowlist: whatever that sandbox can reach, the broker will run. What each
command may touch is one grant set, written in nono's own schema at
`{{.ProfilePath}}`, and it is the same for every command — nothing here gives
one tool a narrower or wider reach than another.
{{if .BrokerIssue}}
{{.BrokerIssue}} — so this document cannot say whether the profile declares
policy-controlled commands after all. Read the absence of tiers below as
"not established", not as "none exist".
{{end}}
A refusal therefore arrives as an ordinary permission failure — `Permission
denied` from the program itself, or exit 127 when the binary cannot be executed
at all — not as a policy denial carrying a reason. Do not read such a failure as
a rule you might phrase around; it means the path or host is outside what the
profile grants. Ask the operator to widen the profile, or work inside what it
already reaches.

Network access goes through nono's proxy and is limited to the domains the
profile lists. A direct TCP connection, a DNS lookup that bypasses the proxy,
and binding a listening port are all refused at the socket layer, so a tool that
ignores the proxy environment variables does not get out — it fails immediately
rather than falling back to an unmediated connection.

A toolchain that can compile and immediately execute code you give it is bounded
only by what that same sandbox reaches, and it can run whatever those grants
cover, including a program it builds itself. The grant set is the real boundary;
there is no dispatch-time check standing behind it.

The broker's own shell builtins (`echo`, `cd`, `test`, `read`, and the others
`mvdan.cc/sh` implements) run inside the broker itself, at those same grants. A
redirect or glob you write is bounded by them too, not by whichever command's
output it is attached to.
{{end}}

What a command may touch is not `agent-sandbox.toml`'s concern — it is the
command profile's, written in nono's own schema, resolved at
`{{.ProfilePath}}`.

## Changing the config
The sandbox is configured by `{{.ConfigPath}}`, which names two nono profiles.
You may edit any of the three files directly.

- Your own sandbox — this process, its file tools, and any MCP server it spawns
  as a direct child — is `{{.AgentProfilePath}}`.
- The sandbox every shell command runs in is `{{.ProfilePath}}`.

Both are written in nono's own schema. agent-sandbox does not generate, read,
or validate either one; it hands each path to nono. So this document cannot
tell you what they grant — nono can:

- `nono profile show <path>` resolves a profile, groups expanded and `~` and
  `$XDG_CACHE_HOME` filled in.
- `nono why --profile <path> --path <p> --op read|write|readwrite` says whether
  an access is allowed and which grant decides it. `--host`, `--command` and
  `--scope` ask the same question of network, command policy, and scope.

One thing neither command reports: environment variables. `nono profile show`
does not print `environment.allow_vars`, so if a variable is not reaching a
process, read that list in the profile file itself. `agent-sandbox doctor`
measures the one variable that matters most — `AGENT_SANDBOX_BROKER_SOCKET`,
without which nothing can reach the command broker at all.

Editing any of this **does not affect the current session**: both profiles are
read once, when the session starts. An edit takes effect the next time the
operator runs `agent-sandbox claude`.

After editing, run `agent-sandbox ai config-check` — it loads the config and
hands both profiles to `nono profile validate`, so a passing check means none
of the three files is what breaks the next launch. `agent-sandbox doctor` goes
further: it adds the broker-socket probe and asks nono whether the command
profile leaves the broker's own binary writable.

Schema:

```toml
tool_mode = "hook"                       # "hook" | "mcp"
command_profile = "command-profile.json" # default name; shared by every agent

[mcp]
command_output_dir = "..."               # mcp tool_mode only: where command output is written

[agents.claude]
profile = "claude-profile.json"          # default name: "<agent>-profile.json"
```

Both profile paths resolve beside `{{.ConfigPath}}` unless written absolute.

A user-scope config at `~/.config/agent-sandbox/config.toml` is composed with
this file: every value the project file sets wins, and an agent the user-scope
file declares alone still applies. If a profile path you did not write shows up
in `agent-sandbox ai config-check`, it comes from there.
